15192459,2025-08-11,Vehicle loan or lease,Loan,Managing the loan or lease,Problem with fees charged,"SUMMARY OF COMPLAINT I, XXXX XXXX XXXX, suffered ascertainable economic damages as a direct and proximate result of NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 's misrepresentations, deceptive practices, misconduct, breach of contract, mail fraud, breach of privacy, insurance fraud, etc., including but not limited to paying excessive interest, loss of expected rebate benefits, and diminished value of the contract bargain. The causes of injury are further described therein : A. - MISREPRESENTATION OF ANNUAL PERCENTAGE RATE ( APR ), MISREPRESENTATION & DECEPTION 1. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY is a creditor as defined under the Truth in Lending Act ( TILA ), 15 U.S.C. 1602 ( g ), and the Texas Finance Code 348.001 ( 3 ), engaged in the business of financing motor vehicle purchases in the State of Texas.

2. On or about XX/XX/year>, I, XXXX XXXX XXXX entered into a Retail Installment Sales Contract ( RISC ) with NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY for the financing of a motor vehicle. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY not only misrepresented that a "" down payment '' was required but al represented in the TILA disclosure statement that the Annual Percentage Rate ( APR ) for my loan was [ stated APR 7 %, to be repaid in [ XXXX ] monthly payments of $ [ XXXX ]. 

3. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY calculated interest not by using a true annual rate, but by applying a daily periodic rate to the outstanding principal balance, compounding interest in a manner that front-loaded interest charges and extended the time in which I would pay interest. This is not only deceptive but would be a considered "" breach of contract '' due to the extension and/or modification beyond the mutually agreed terms of the contract.

4. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY failed to disclose that the stated APR did not reflect the actual cost of credit unless I, XXXX XXXX XXXX, made substantially greater ( double ) payment, XXXX portion toward principal and one toward interest. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY also failed to disclose that if I, XXXX XXXX XXXX, made only the scheduled payments, I would ( a ) indefinitely remain behind on accrued interest near the end of the loan term, and ( b ) not receive the purported interest rebate without making such extra payments. Again, this is not only deceptive but would be a considered "" breach of contract '' due to the extension and/or modification beyond the mutually agreed terms of the contract.

5. As a result of this calculation method and nondisclosure that is indeed absent from the "" Itemization List '' within the contract, the actual cost of credit exceeded the stated APR, causing the disclosed APR to be inaccurate within the meaning of 12 C.F.R. 1026.22.

NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 's conduct constitutes : A violation of the Truth in Lending Act, 15 U.S.C. 1638 ( a ) ( 4 ), which requires creditors to accurately disclose the APR as the annualized cost of credit actually imposed ; A violation of Regulation Z, 12 C.F.R. 1026.22 ( a ), which prohibits the disclosure of an inaccurate APR ; An unfair or deceptive act or practice under the Federal Trade Commission Act, 15 U.S.C. 45 ( a ) ; A violation of the Texas Deceptive Trade PracticesConsumer Protection Act ( DTPA ), Tex. Bus. & Com. Code 17.46 ( b ) ( 5 ), ( 7 ), and ( 12 ), by : Misrepresenting the characteristics, uses, and benefits of financing services ; Representing that the agreement conferred rights or obligations it did not in fact confer ; and A violation 15 U.S. Code 1662 - Advertising of downpayments and installments - No advertisement to aid, promote, or assist directly or indirectly any extension of consumer credit may state ( 1 ) that a specific periodic consumer credit amount or installment amount can be arranged, unless the creditor usually and customarily arranges credit payments or installments for that period and in that amount.

( 2 ) that a specified downpayment is required in connection with any extension of consumer credit, unless the creditor usually and customarily arranges downpayments in that amount.

NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 'S Failure to disclose information concerning the financing that was known at the time of the transaction triggered inducement into a transaction I would not have entered into had the information been disclosed.

B. - THEFT BY DECEPTION, BREACH OF PRIVACY RIGHTS & PERMISSIBLE PURPOSES - Facts to support complaint When an auto loan is originated, the creditor ( NISSAN MOTOR FINANCE COMPANY ) often bundles multiple consumer 's signed retail installment contract with many others into an investment product called an Asset-Backed Security ( ABS ).

These bundles are sold to investors through a trust or special purpose vehicle ( SPV ).

The contract is no longer just a private agreement between you and the original lender it becomes a financial instrument traded in capital markets. ( Separating the note from the bond. ) Embedded in these contracts lies our personal identifiers ( name, address, SSN, income, payment history ) and financial details ( loan amount, interest rate, collateral info ).

1. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY failed to provide Consumer Disclosure and/or Informed Consent.

2. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY failed to provide Consumer Disclosure and/or Informed consent within the reviewing and signing of the Retail Installment Sales Contract ( RISC ), ( TILA ) Truth in Lending disclosures within the contract do not explicitly explain that the contract will be sold as part of a pooled security to third parties who are not providing direct credit to XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX 

3. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY failed to provide Consumer Disclosure and/or Informed consent within the reviewing and signing of the Retail Installment Sales Contract ( RISC ), I, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX was led to believe the relationship was solely between myself and NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY , not an unknown network of investors.

4. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 's converted Consumer Identity into a Tradeable Commodity.

NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY failed to provide Consumer Disclosure of the loan contract being converted into a security without express permission.

Myself and millions of others, identity and financial obligations are effectively monetized through a pool of unknown investors..

This would constitute as enrichment profiting from your personal and financial data without legal basis or compensation to the consumer.

5. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY not only stated that I owed a debt but also used language and symbols on an envelope in communication by mail indicating that they were in the debt collection business and/or that the communication related to the collection of a debt ; NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 's conduct constitutes : Misrepresentation and violation of Permissible Purpose under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) Permissible purposes to access or share your personal credit information are narrowly defined in 15 U.S.C. 1681b ( e.g., evaluating your credit application, servicing your account, complying with a court order ).

Violation of Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) 15 U.S.C. 16811681x 1681b Limits access to consumer reports to specific permissible purposes.

1681e Requires users of consumer information to maintain reasonable procedures to ensure compliance.

Investors in securitized pools are not users with a permissible purpose, yet receive embedded credit data.

A violation of Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act ( GLBA ) 15 U.S.C. 68016809 Requires financial institutions to give notice and obtain consent before sharing nonpublic personal information with non-affiliated third parties.

Pooling and selling ABS with consumer identifiers constitutes disclosure without proper opt-out or consent.

A violation of Truth in Lending Act ( TILA ) 15 U.S.C. 16011667f Mandates accurate disclosure of the creditors identity and assignment of contracts.

Failure to disclose that the creditor will immediately transfer your loan into a securitization trust is a material misrepresentation.

A violation of Identity Theft 18 U.S.C. 1028 & 1028A Which criminalizes knowingly transferring, possessing, or using a means of identification without lawful authority.

Selling a consumer 's personal identifiers as part of an investment security without explicit authority brings forth the element of without lawful authority.

A violation of Texas Business & Commerce Code 521.052 Restricts disclosure of sensitive personal information.

